' The stage set: the control instances the documentation's samples assume.
'
' A sample that says `Text1.Text = "hi"` is form code-behind. It is complete as
' documentation -- the reader has a form with a TextBox on it -- and it cannot
' compile on its own, because the designer, not the code, is what declares
' Text1. Declaring those instances here lets the compiler check what the sample
' is actually asserting: that the member exists, that it takes those arguments,
' that the types line up.
'
' Every name here was measured, not guessed: it is an identifier that appears in
' a `tb` fence under docs/ in the position of a control instance. The list is
' the tool's one deliberate fiction, so it stays explicit and greppable -- an
' inference rule over "a name ending in a digit" would also have declared Var1,
' Arg1, Line2, SQLITE3, VBA7 and MySub1, none of which is a control.
'
' Public, not Private: the samples are generated into their own modules and have
' to see these from there.

Module tbxStage

    Public Form1 As Form
    Public Form2 As Form
    Public Form3 As Form
    Public MDIForm1 As MDIForm
    Public UserForm1 As Form
    Public UserForm2 As Form

    Public Text1 As TextBox
    Public Text2 As TextBox
    Public TextBox1 As TextBox

    Public Label1 As Label
    Public Timer1 As Timer
    Public Command1 As CommandButton
    Public Frame1 As Frame
    Public Picture1 As PictureBox
    Public PictureBox1 As PictureBox

    ' The VB control instances the reference pages put on their forms. Each is
    ' named after the page that assumes it -- ListBox/index.md says List1, and
    ' the three file-system controls share Drive1 / Dir1 / File1 because the
    ' pages show them wired to each other.
    Public List1 As ListBox
    Public Combo1 As ComboBox
    Public Check1 As CheckBox
    Public Drive1 As DriveListBox
    Public Dir1 As DirListBox
    Public File1 As FileListBox
    Public Data1 As Data
    Public OLE1 As OLE

    ' The DataObject pages' samples enumerate a drag-and-drop payload, which in
    ' real code arrives as an OLEDrag handler's `Data` parameter. Declaring it
    ' shadows nothing: `Data1 As Data` above still resolves the VB control class
    ' of the same name, which the compiler was asked directly.
    Public Data As DataObject

    ' The same, where the page uses a descriptive name rather than a numbered one.
    Public picCanvas As PictureBox
    Public hsbVolume As HScrollBar
    Public vsbLevel As VScrollBar
    Public lblStatus As Label
    Public lblCoords As Label
    Public lblClock As Label
    Public cmdOK As CommandButton
    Public optHTML As OptionButton
    Public mnuFileSave As Menu
    Public imgLogo As Image
    Public linUnderHeading As Line
    Public shpPanel As Shape
    Public mfPanels As MultiFrame

    ' UserControl code-behind reaches two intrinsic objects the way form
    ' code-behind reaches Me: the control itself, and its container's ambient
    ' properties. Both are declared here for the same reason Text1 is -- the
    ' designer, not the code, is what brings them into scope.
    Public UserControl As UserControl
    Public Ambient As AmbientProperties

End Module
